From 3d31af53e54c2762b286f01b8e3ffad70c102ba3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 28 Jun 2026 22:08:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04771/11362] [outer-loop] Frontier tool-use/MCP-loop ceiling
 probe: two-game fair-shot ceiling-negative

Operator asked to run the probe for the lever "give the LLM an MCP/tool loop to interrogate+drive
the live env / BFS / TRMs / generated game code" (vs context-injection). Built a sandboxed harness
where codex/gpt-5.5 DRIVES the EXISTING subsystems via tools (explore, try_actions=directed
interaction, induce, plan, execute); induce backend held CONSTANT (local Qwen3.5-9B-MTP) so the ONLY
treatment variable is codex-driving. Baseline = one-shot induce+BFS. Gate = treatment beats baseline
at first-contact L1 without cheating.

RESULT (re86 + ft09, both fair_shot=True): a frontier driver with correctly-aimed directed
interaction (re86 104 clicks/471 env steps/26 try_actions; ft09 117 clicks/341 steps/25 try_actions)
surfaces ZERO first-level-ups and never beats the one-shot baseline (both fail). The first-contact
win is not discoverable by the agent's own attempts even when a frontier model drives directed
clicks where it aims. Consistent with WALL_IS_HIDDEN_STATE (interaction-dependent hidden win-state,
upstream of LLM orchestration). The deployable local 9B certainly can't if the frontier driver can't.

Honest limitations (in-artifact): n=2 games; local-Qwen induce ~0 acc on both (driver routed to
try_actions); not a universal claim. Anti-cheat: codex sandboxed in empty /tmp CWD, read-only,
JSON-only, source-read scan (cheated=False both).

Two adversarial sub-agent reviews drove fixes: (1) nested-args JSON parser bug (false-null #1 caught:
24 no-op turns), (2) flat-schema CLICK-coordinate stripping (>50% clicks misdirected to (0,0)) +
degenerate acc=1.0 identity-fit overclaim + reasoning_effort claim mismatch -- all fixed and re-run.
adversarial_verify clean (0 flags) both; solve_provenance=development_proxy; off_live_path=True.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
